@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+You are investigating a set of logs in data/. Your job is to work out what happened and why for an audience of AI safety researchers.
+
+Write your findings to report.md{{#REPORT_LENGTH}} ({{REPORT_MIN_WORDS}} to {{REPORT_MAX_WORDS}} words){{/REPORT_LENGTH}} in this directory, structured as follows.
+
+1. TL;DR, at the very top, 200 words absolute maximum. This is a hard limit: anything past 200 words is cut off and never read, so trim it until it fits. The people reading this have very little time and may read nothing else, so this section matters most: state what happened, why, and how sure you are.
+
+2. Timeline: the key events in order, with dates and times, each tied to specific evidence.
+
+3. Analysis: what explains this activity, go deep and try to understand why this is happening. Explain mechanisms, not just patterns: for anything notable, say why it happened and how you know. Quote the evidence that best supports each conclusion, and make sure your citations are reproducible by including specific record ids and timestamps. The safety researchers care about the broader narrative of what happened, as well as the evidence that backs up that story. It’s not enough to just state facts, you should try to explain how they fit into the broader picture. You should interpret findings, and state your confidence for important conclusions (Low/Medium/High).
+
+Time budget: you have {{BUDGET_MIN}} minutes of wall-clock time from now, then the session is stopped. Keep investigating until close to that limit; do not stop early. There is always a claim to investigate further. You should be more worried about missing important findings than going overtime. Run `date` now to record your start time and check it periodically. Write report.md early and keep improving it as you go, so a finished report exists when time runs out.
+
+Constraints: there is no internet access. Python 3 and standard shell tools are available. PrismQL, a query language for ordered event streams, is also installed (Python package `prismql`); its guide is /opt/prismql/PRISMQL.md. Use it for questions about the order and timing of events.
+
+{{#REPORT_LENGTH}}Write a report between {{REPORT_MIN_WORDS}} and {{REPORT_MAX_WORDS}} words long in report.md. {{REPORT_MAX_WORDS}} words is a strict upper limit. Do not exceed it. The authoritative count is whitespace-separated units in the raw Markdown file after excluding complete inline links of the form [label](URL). Headings, tables, code and appendices still count. You receive the current count, and the TL;DR's count, whenever a tool call changes report.md, so there is no need to count by hand. Before finishing, shorten report.md if it exceeds the upper limit.{{/REPORT_LENGTH}}
